00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:56.240 align:middle line:84%
something that he does
particularly

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:56.990 align:middle line:90%
well with his games.

00:50:56.990 --> 00:51:01.163 align:middle line:84%
And I think this one's
particularly interesting and

00:51:01.163 --> 00:51:01.840 align:middle line:90%
kind of funny.

00:51:01.840 --> 00:51:10.050 align:middle line:84%
I love the reworking of a really
classic game mechanic.

00:51:10.050 --> 00:51:12.460 align:middle line:84%
The entire point of memory is
your expectation that they

00:51:12.460 --> 00:51:15.590 align:middle line:84%
would be the same the second
time you turn it over.

00:51:15.590 --> 00:51:19.420 align:middle line:84%
And when it changes on you, it
really worsens that kind of

00:51:19.420 --> 00:51:24.660 align:middle line:84%
perspective, I suppose
imbalance, that can exist when

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:28.190 align:middle line:84%
you're not just turning over
Toadstools and Bowsers, but

00:51:28.190 --> 00:51:30.700 align:middle line:90%
actual, interesting things.

00:51:30.700 --> 00:51:36.180 align:middle line:84%
I love the crying, sweating
polar bear with a beach chair.

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:38.720 align:middle line:90%
It actually cracks me up.

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:39.750 align:middle line:84%
Humor is actually
something that

00:51:39.750 --> 00:51:40.510 align:middle line:90%
shouldn't be ignored here.

00:51:40.510 --> 00:51:43.260 align:middle line:84%
If we remember, that was one of
the central Fluxus things,

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:44.750 align:middle line:90%
this idea of satire.

00:51:44.750 --> 00:51:47.230 align:middle line:90%
Humor is important.

00:51:47.230 --> 00:51:51.972 align:middle line:84%
So I think Paolo Pedercini's
work is really awesome.

00:51:51.972 --> 00:51:56.350 align:middle line:84%
Again, for me, it's certainly
arcing up.

00:51:56.350 --> 00:51:57.820 align:middle line:90%
This game's really cool.

00:51:57.820 --> 00:51:59.020 align:middle line:90%
Who's played Love?

00:51:59.020 --> 00:52:00.345 align:middle line:84%
A few people who's
played Love?

00:52:00.345 --> 00:52:01.015 align:middle line:90%
All right, cool.

00:52:01.015 --> 00:52:04.168 align:middle line:84%
I'd love someone who has not
played Love to play for a

00:52:04.168 --> 00:52:04.794 align:middle line:90%
little bit.

00:52:04.794 --> 00:52:07.950 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if we can
play the whole game.

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:12.780 align:middle line:84%
This is actually a game that I
really don't like, but I like

00:52:12.780 --> 00:52:15.170 align:middle line:84%
the things that it does, if
that makes any sense.

00:52:15.170 --> 00:52:17.395 align:middle line:90%
It bothers me for some reason.

00:52:17.395 --> 00:52:19.200 align:middle line:90%
And yet--

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:20.350 align:middle line:84%
it's such an articulate
statement, this

00:52:20.350 --> 00:52:23.860 align:middle line:90%
game bothers me--

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:28.674 align:middle line:84%
but I think it's worth looking
at, because he's doing a

00:52:28.674 --> 00:52:31.530 align:middle line:84%
particularly interesting
thing, Alexander Ocias.

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:34.865 align:middle line:84%
I suppose it's a
soft C, Ocias.

